Welcome and reset (5 min): They'll already be warmed up from the factory, so keep this short. Share something small and human yourself, such as a mistake you made as a junior. Say up front that there are no wrong answers today and anyone can pass at any point.

Part 1: Psychological safety (20 min)

Psychological safety is the shared belief that it's safe to take interpersonal risks in a team: asking a question, admitting a mistake, or challenging an idea without being embarrassed or punished. The term comes from Amy Edmondson's research, and Google's Project Aristotle found it was the strongest predictor of effective teams.

Short input (10 min)

Keep this conversational rather than lecture-style. Cover three points:
1. What it is: Feeling able to say "I don't know", "I got that wrong", or "I'm not sure that's right" without worrying how it'll land.
2. What it isn't: It's not about being nice all the time, lowering standards, or avoiding hard conversations. Safe teams disagree more openly, not less.
3. Why it matters for engineers: Bugs get raised early, code reviews are honest, incidents get learned from rather than blamed, and juniors ask for help before they're stuck for a day.

Draw Edmondson's two-by-two on the flipchart: psychological safety on one axis, accountability (high standards) on the other. Low safety and low standards is apathy; high safety and low standards is comfort; low safety and high standards is anxiety; high on both is the learning zone. Ask: "Which box do you think most apprentices start in, and why?"

Table activity: Factory debrief (7 min)

Tables sit in their factory teams and talk through these questions. Ask them to focus on what happened, not who did it.
– When did you have an idea or spot a problem? Did you say it out loud?
– What made it easier or harder to speak up while the clock was running?
– Who did most of the talking, and how did quieter people get heard?
– When something went wrong, how did the team react?
– If you ran the factory again, what's one thing you'd change about how you worked together?

Debrief (3 min): Ask two or three tables for one moment where someone did or didn't speak up, and place it on the two-by-two. Did time pressure push anyone into the anxiety zone? Draw out that small, everyday behaviours do most of the work, and that apprentices can build safety too, not just leads.

Part 2: Communication styles and preferences (25 min)

Everyone has preferences about how they communicate and work, and none of them are wrong; friction usually comes from assuming others work the way we do. Steer away from personality-type labels and talk about preferences on a spectrum, which can shift with context.

Short input (5 min)

Show these spectrums on a slide and ask people to notice where they sit on each, without sharing yet:
