Solo reflection: personal user manual (10 min)

Each apprentice fills in the "How to work with me" handout (template at the end of this doc). Remind them it's theirs to keep and they choose what to share.

Pairs (10 min)

In pairs, each person shares two or three things from their manual. The listener asks one question: "What would it look like if someone got this wrong for you?" Then swap. Encourage pairs to spot where they differ, as that's the useful bit for Part 3.

Part 3: 1-2-4-All on ways of working (30 min)

1-2-4-All is a Liberating Structures format that gets everyone contributing, not just the confident voices. People think alone, then build ideas up in pairs, then fours, then share the best with the whole room. Run it twice with two different prompts.

How to run each round (12 min)
1. 1 (1 min): Silent, solo reflection. Everyone writes their own ideas on sticky notes, one idea per note.
2. 2 (2 min): Pairs share and build on each other's ideas, looking for similarities and differences.
3. 4 (4 min): Two pairs join. Share, then agree on the one or two ideas that stand out most.
4. All (5 min): Each group of four shares one standout idea that hasn't already been heard. Capture each on the flipchart.

Keep timings tight and visible; the pace is part of the format. Use a chime or raised hand to move between stages rather than talking over people.

Round 1 prompt (blue sticky notes)

What do you need from your teammates to feel comfortable asking questions, sharing ideas, and admitting mistakes?

Round 2 prompt (yellow sticky notes)

When someone's working style or communication preference is different from yours, what could you practically do to make it work for both of you?

Nudge towards specific behaviours over general values. "Be respectful" is fine as a start, but ask "What would that look like on a Tuesday afternoon?" to get to "Ask before calling someone" or "Put the key point at the top of a message."

Transition (3 min)

With the group's help, quickly cluster the flipchart ideas into themes, such as meetings, messages, feedback, asking for help, and focus time. These clusters become the raw material for the working agreement.

Part 4: Working agreement and close (15 min)

Turn the clustered ideas into a short team working agreement the apprentices own. Aim for six to eight statements, each one a behaviour starting with "We will..." or "We'll...".

Drafting the agreement (10 min)
1. Read the clusters back to the room.
2. For each cluster, ask the group to propose one "We'll..." statement. Write it up as they say it, then tidy the wording together.
3. Do a quick fist-to-five on each statement (fist means "I can't support this", five means "Fully agree"). Anything at two or below gets a quick conversation or is parked.

Example statements to prompt with if the room is quiet:
– We'll say "I don't know" and ask for help within 30 minutes of being stuck
– We'll check how someone prefers to receive feedback before giving it
– We'll put the main point or ask at the top of messages
– We'll respect focus time and use async messages unless it's urgent
– We'll give everyone a chance to speak before a decision is made

Close (5 min)

Ask each person for one word on how they're leaving the session, or one thing they'll try this week. Explain what happens next: you'll type up the agreement and share it, and the group will revisit it at a set point (for example, after their first sprint and again around day 30).

Facilitator notes

The biggest thing you can do is model the behaviour you're teaching: admit what you don't know, thank people for questions, and react calmly when someone challenges you.
– Make sharing optional. Preferences can touch on neurodivergence, disability, anxiety, or caring responsibilities. Never ask anyone to explain why they prefer something, and let people pass at any point.
– Watch for quieter voices. The solo minute in 1-2-4-All helps, but also notice who hasn't spoken in the "All" stage and invite tables, not individuals, to share.
– Avoid labelling people. If someone says "I'm just an introvert", gently reframe to the specific preference, such as "So you'd rather have time to think before a meeting?"
– Hold the line on accountability. If the conversation drifts towards "safety means nobody criticises my code", bring back the two-by-two: the goal is the learning zone, where safety and high standards sit together.
– Mixed cohorts. Career-changers may have strong habits from previous workplaces; invite them to share what worked there, as it's valuable for the group.
– Remote version. Use breakout rooms for the 2 and 4 stages, and a shared board with columns for each prompt. Keep cameras optional.

On the day: Run the paper plane factory first, straight after lunch, then this session. During the factory, jot down a few moments worth revisiting (someone spotting an issue, a handoff going wrong, a quiet voice with a good idea) so you can prompt tables in the Part 1 debrief if they get stuck.

Follow-up
– [ ] Type up and share the working agreement within a couple of days
– [ ] Encourage apprentices to share their personal user manual with their buddy or line manager, if they're comfortable
– [ ] Revisit the agreement after the first sprint and around day 30
– [ ] Ask for quick anonymous feedback on the session

